@startuml fig-18-estop-timing-budget
' =====================================================================
' Figure 18. What "the surgeon can stop it" costs in milliseconds
' Type            : PlantUML-type, timing diagram (robust and concise clocks)
' Paper section   : § 7, What Happens in the Operating Room
' Concern answered: ChatGPT concern 3 (human override and rescue capability)
'                   and concern 1 (safety, malfunction, unintended actions);
'                   Gemini family 2. "The surgeon can always stop it" is the
'                   most common reassurance offered to patients and the least
'                   informative, because it does not say how long stopping
'                   takes. This figure puts the whole chain on a millisecond
'                   axis and shows every budget line the protocol commits to.
' Why this type   : A timing diagram is the only formal notation with time as a
'                   metric axis rather than an ordering. The argument here is
'                   quantitative, so the axis has to be quantitative.
'
' TikZ: draw with the uml* vocabulary of patientstyle.sty.
' TikZ: Five stacked timelines, each a 14 cm horizontal band at
' TikZ:   y = 0, -1.6, -3.2, -4.8, -6.4, with the state name anchored east in
' TikZ:   a 3.4 cm left gutter in \tiny\sffamily.
' TikZ: The x axis is logarithmic in milliseconds from 0.1 to 1000, with decade
' TikZ:   ticks at 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000 in protogray 0.4pt and \tiny labels
' TikZ:   beneath the lowest band.
' TikZ: A state segment is a 4mm-tall rectangle; filled protoblue while the
' TikZ:   signal is asserted, pablue2 while armed, pagraym while idle.
' TikZ: Transitions are vertical 0.5pt protoblack rules between bands, drawn at
' TikZ:   the transition instant, never diagonal.
' TikZ: Budget markers are 0.8pt protoblue dashed verticals spanning all five
' TikZ:   bands, labelled at the top in \tiny\sffamily\bfseries inside a
' TikZ:   white-filled mmlabel node so the label never sits on a band.
' TikZ: No curved connectors in this figure, so no looseness is required.
' =====================================================================

!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam ArrowColor #6C757D
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #E9ECEF
skinparam NoteBorderColor #6C757D

scale 1 as 40 pixels

robust "Surgeon intent" as SUR
robust "Heartbeat bus (10 kHz)" as BUS
robust "Arm 1 to 8 motion" as ARM
robust "Force at the tip" as FRC
concise "Protocol budget line" as BUD

SUR has Operating, Stop_pressed
BUS has Nominal, Halt_broadcast, Quiescent
ARM has Commanded, Decelerating, Held
FRC has Within_cap, Falling, Zero

@0
SUR is Operating
BUS is Nominal
ARM is Commanded
FRC is Within_cap
BUD is "steady state"

@0.1
SUR is Stop_pressed
BUD is "t = 0, stop asserted"

@0.2
BUS is Halt_broadcast
BUD is "one bus tick, 0.1 ms period"

@1.4
ARM is Decelerating
BUD is "arms accept halt"

@2.8
FRC is Falling

@3.0
ARM is Held
BUS is Quiescent
BUD is "cross-arm budget: 3 ms"

@4.2
FRC is Zero

@50
BUD is "system-wide budget: 500 ms"

@60
SUR is Operating
BUD is "surgeon resumes or converts"

highlight 0.1 to 3.0 #DCE8F1 : cross-arm halt, budget 3 ms
highlight 3.0 to 50 #E9ECEF : remaining system-wide margin, budget 500 ms

note bottom
  Three budget lines, all protocol-specified, none aspirational:
  heartbeat period 0.1 ms; cross-arm emergency stop at most 3 ms;
  system-wide emergency stop at most 500 ms. The gap between the
  cross-arm halt and the system-wide budget is deliberate margin,
  not unaccounted latency.
end note

note top
  For scale: a human blink is roughly 100 to 150 ms.
  Every arm is held before a blink completes, and the
  system-wide guarantee is under four blinks.
end note

@enduml
